9.4.0
2026-07-13
track-changes v9.4.0 — region acceptance: paragraph continuity (additive, opt-in).
- PROBLEM: a region carved from inside a paragraph (a mid/end sentence pulled out to
  source or mark it) sits as its own block; on `/tc accept` the wrapper is stripped but
  the block's blank-line boundaries remain, leaving the sentence as a standalone
  paragraph instead of rejoining the one it came from — right text, wrong paragraphing,
  silently.
- `tc-join` (opt-in, the proper fix): a region may declare that it rejoins an adjacent
  paragraph on accept — md/qmd `::: {.tc-region … tc-join="prev"}` (merge into the
  PRECEDING paragraph) or `tc-join="next"`; LaTeX a 4th optional on `tcregion`,
  `\begin{tcregion}{N}[prov][src][join]` (arg spec `m o o o`, back-compatible — the
  macro accepts and render-ignores it). On accept the body is spliced onto the end
  (`prev`) / start (`next`) of the neighbouring paragraph with a single space. The
  neighbour scan skips gray `.tc-verbatim` scaffolding (so a sourced region rejoins the
  paragraph ABOVE its excerpt block, and a `prev` merge subsumes that gray block).
  Absent the attribute, resolution is byte-for-byte as in 9.3.0. No valid neighbour
  (BOF/EOF or a structural neighbour) ⇒ fall back to the standalone strip + a note.
- Accept-time orphan warning (always-on safety net): after `/tc accept`, an advisory
  WARNING (never a block; exit unchanged) fires when a resolved region left a
  single-line paragraph sandwiched between two body paragraphs — the region-split
  signature — suggesting `tc-join`. A joined region, or one resolved at a real
  structural boundary, does not trigger it.
- Additive audit field `join: prev|next` on joined regions. Back-compatible grammar; no
  new hooks (suite stays 6); no document migration.

